A direct digit-by-digit loop is enough here. Take the last digit with `% 10`, add it to the result, and remove it from the original number with `/ 10`. The `long long` variable lets the code detect an `int` overflow before returning.

For example, `1234` becomes `4`, then `43`, then `432`, and finally `4321`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int reverseNumber(int n) {
        long long value = n;
        long long reversed = 0;

        while (value != 0) {
            int digit = value % 10;
            reversed = reversed * 10 + digit;
            value /= 10;
        }

        if (reversed < -2147483648LL || reversed > 2147483647LL) {
            return 0;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(reversed);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`, where `d` is the number of digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The helper removes one digit at a time and places it at the end of a growing result. Recursion makes the digit order easy to follow: the last digit is added first, then the next one.

A `long` is used while building the answer. The original `int` value can be negative, so `Math.abs` is applied after converting to `long`, which also handles `Integer.MIN_VALUE` safely.

Dry run for `n = -507`: the helper builds `705`, and the outer method changes it to `-705`.

Edge cases such as `0`, a negative number, and an overflowing answer are covered.

```java
class Solution {
    public int reverseNumber(int n) {
        long reversed = reverseDigits(Math.abs((long) n), 0L);

        if (n < 0) {
            reversed = -reversed;
        }

        if (reversed < Integer.MIN_VALUE || reversed > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
            return 0;
        }

        return (int) reversed;
    }

    private long reverseDigits(long value, long reversed) {
        if (value == 0) {
            return reversed;
        }

        return reverseDigits(value / 10, reversed * 10 + value % 10);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` for the recursive call stack.

The digits are collected from right to left, then used from left to right with place values `1`, `10`, `100`, and so on. This makes the change in place value visible instead of building the number with multiplication inside the extraction loop.

For `n = 246`, the extracted digits are `[6, 4, 2]`. Reading that list backward gives `2*100 + 4*10 + 6`, which is `246` in reverse order, or `642` when the list is used in its stored order from the highest place.

The calculation uses `int64` so overflow can be checked before converting back to `int`.

```go
func reverseNumber(n int) int {
    negative := n < 0
    value := int64(n)
    if value < 0 {
        value = -value
    }

    digits := make([]int64, 0)
    if value == 0 {
        digits = append(digits, 0)
    }

    for value > 0 {
        digits = append(digits, value%10)
        value /= 10
    }

    var reversed int64
    place := int64(1)
    for i := len(digits) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
        reversed += digits[i] * place
        place *= 10
    }

    if negative {
        reversed = -reversed
    }

    const maxInt = int64(^uint(0) >> 1)
    minInt := -maxInt - 1
    if reversed < minInt || reversed > maxInt {
        return 0
    }

    return int(reversed)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` for the stored digits.

Instead of changing the result while scanning, this version first stores every digit in a list. The list is then read from the last digit to the first one to create the reversed value.

Using `long` for the rebuilt number prevents overflow during the calculation. A value outside the `int` range returns `0`.

The input number is not changed because integers are value types in C#.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int ReverseNumber(int n)
    {
        bool negative = n < 0;
        long value = Math.Abs((long)n);
        List<int> digits = new List<int>();

        if (value == 0)
        {
            digits.Add(0);
        }

        while (value > 0)
        {
            digits.Add((int)(value % 10));
            value /= 10;
        }

        long reversed = 0;
        for (int i = digits.Count - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            reversed = reversed * 10 + digits[i];
        }

        if (negative)
        {
            reversed = -reversed;
        }

        if (reversed < int.MinValue || reversed > int.MaxValue)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        return (int)reversed;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`.
Space complexity: `O(d)` for the digit list.

class Solution {
    public int reverseNumber(int n) {
        int rev = 0;

        while (n != 0) {
            int rem = n % 10;

            // Overflow check
            if (rev > Integer.MAX_VALUE / 10 ||
               (rev == Integer.MAX_VALUE / 10 && rem > 7)) {
                return 0;
            }
            if (rev < Integer.MIN_VALUE / 10 ||
               (rev == Integer.MIN_VALUE / 10 && rem < -8)) {
                return 0;
            }

            rev = rev * 10 + rem;
            n /= 10;
        }
        return rev;
    }
}

